diff --git a/docs/UPGRADE.md b/docs/UPGRADE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e041b3f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/UPGRADE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,71 @@
+# Runtime upgrade plan
+
+## Ruby 3.2 / Rails 6.1 release
+
+Production in this release is Ruby 3.2.9 and Rails 6.1.7.10. The default
+`Gemfile` is the production bundle. `Gemfile.next` explicitly selects Rails
+7.0.8.7 for validation and the following release; CI runs the full test suite
+with both bundles on Ruby 3.2.
+
+The `secret_key_base`, `_redmine_session` cookie name, cookie path, and mail
+link verifier purpose must remain unchanged across every deployment and
+rollback. Only the framework-generated representation of a cookie changes.
+
+This Rails 6.1 release retains Redmine's existing behavior except for the cookie
+settings needed to cross the version boundary. It enables authenticated
+encrypted cookies and cookie metadata; Rails' cookie rotation transparently
+decrypts cookies issued before those settings were enabled. The Rails 6.1
+bundle uses the `:hybrid` cookie serializer: it reads the existing Marshal
+sessions and rewrites them as JSON.
+
+The Rails 7 bundle loads the Rails 7.0 framework defaults with a temporary
+`:hybrid` serializer override. This accepts Marshal cookies from users who do
+not visit during the Rails 6.1 rollout. `:hybrid` writes JSON exactly as Rails
+7's default serializer does, so current sessions are readable in both upgrade
+and rollback directions without assuming every user has first visited the
+Rails 6.1 release.
+
+Nested values stored in sessions are now indifferent-access hashes. Query state
+and external-auth registration therefore survive both Marshal and JSON
+serialization. Rails 7 date fixture formatting uses `to_formatted_s`, and the
+API template format, template rendering, and same-host referer redirects use
+Rails 7-compatible paths without changing Redmine's API or link behavior.
+
+Rails 7.0's `belongs_to_required_by_default` setting remains disabled because
+Redmine's existing optional-association semantics are part of the domain model,
+not a Rails migration step. New associations should declare whether they are
+optional explicitly. This setting is not a rollback-only compatibility shim.
+
+`Rails.application.message_verifier(:mail_links)` uses the SHA1-derived Rails
+6.1 key. Rails 7 normally derives application keys with SHA256, which would
+invalidate existing 30-day mail-link tokens. Rails 7 therefore temporarily
+keeps `active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class` on SHA1. This override
+also makes Rails 7 session cookies readable by the Rails 6.1 rollback bundle.
+
+Rails 7's cache format is incompatible with Rails 6.1, so Rails 7 temporarily
+keeps `active_support.cache_format_version` at 6.1. Cache misses after a rollback
+are acceptable; unreadable cache values are not written in this migration
+window.
+
+## Next release
+
+Deploy `Gemfile.next` to run Rails 7.0 on the Rails 7.0 framework defaults,
+subject to the three rollback overrides above. Keep Ruby at 3.2 and upgrade no
+other production runtime in that release. Do not rotate the signing key or
+change cookie serialization during that release. Promote the Rails 7 manifest
+to the production default only after this dual-bundle suite passes with the
+production database adapters and plugins.
+
+Before a later release removes the rollback overrides, the Rails 6.1 rollback
+window must have ended. Enabling the SHA256 key generator then requires
+explicit rotations for old session cookies and the `:mail_links` verifier;
+previous mail tokens must be allowed to expire for 30 days first. Switching to
+cache format 7.0 should be paired with a cache namespace/version change or
+cache flush. Remove the `:hybrid` override only after rollout telemetry or a
+separate soak period confirms old Marshal session cookies no longer need to be
+accepted.
+
+Rails 7 deprecations for `ActiveRecord::Base.default_timezone` and implicit
+`SafeBuffer` string conversion remain work for the following Rails release;
+they do not change behavior on Rails 7.0. Re-run and fix those deprecations
+before preparing Rails 7.1 or a later framework-default update.
